Although just because your formats, grammar, and information about the SCP are long, that doesn't mean your SCP would be rated high, trust me, there were SCPs that have less information that got accepted into the wiki. Such as SCP-5924, information, and special containment procedures of it was lesser than yours, yet there were no incident nor experimental logs at all, just one, certain interview log, and only 3 footnotes.

http://www.scpwiki.com/scp-5924

As I said, it doesn't matter whether if it's informative, what matters is all of the writing skills you have, if it's bad your SCP would get downrated vastly fast. And believe me when I said that, because a few days ago, I've written an SCP that contains a lot of information, even logs, but it got downrated vastly fast without even wasting away an hour.

Another thing is about the person who is interviewing your SCP, researchers of the foundation have always interacted with a lot of abnormal SCPs from superhumans (including psychopathic, and evil types) to humanoid monsters (including the ones that have bad intentions on humans) that understand human languages, the Dr that you've put into the log was shown to be afraid, but that's impossible because every researcher and interviewers of the foundation always have confidence and the courage in interacting eye to eye with the SCPs.





